g at the end. The game was close, with Black's lead between -0.1 and -3.3 through move 10, until GPT-6.1 Sol's 11 D5 lost 5.1 points and 15 D4 lost 16.4, taking the lead to -25.1. The 20k's own 18 D3 gave back 18.7 points, but GPT-6.1 Sol could not recover the rest, and the endgame stayed near -12.", "moments": [{"turn": 5, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's E5 lost 3.0 points, taking the lead from +0.0 to -2.9. The engine preferred F6, and the opening drifted from there: five of Black's first six moves lost 1.5 points or more."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "D5 lost 5.1 points, taking the lead from -3.3 to -8.4. The engine's choice was F4. White then took F4 and, a few moves later, E4, pushing into Black's wall from below."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "D4 was the game's biggest blunder, losing 16.4 points and dropping the lead from -8.7 to -25.1. The engine wanted E3. White took E3 and cut Black's stones into separate groups."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 20k answered with D3, which lost 18.7 points and moved the lead from -27.8 back to -9.1. The engine's choice was D2. It was the biggest swing of the game, and it gave back nearly all of Black's earlier blunder."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "C4 lost 4.2 points, from -8.1 to -12.3, and the engine preferred J4. By the final position Black had a wall along row 6 and the bottom left, but White's stones on row 7 and in the right-hand centre held enough area for the 12-point win."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran parses the board text, lists chains, liberties and empty regions, audits the single move the player named (captures, suicide), replays the finished game and writes notes, SGF and logs. The 'audit' function only places one stone the player picked and never loops over candidates or plays a sequence forward, so nothing chose or ranked moves.", "stop": false}}